WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  growing up i desperately wanted to feel  a sense of connection and belonging  as an asian american raised in new canon  connecticut there were very few people  of color and even fewer who looked like  me  and i didn't need to look myself in the  mirror to see that i was different  instead my classmates reminded me of it  every single day  slant their eyes at me in the hallway  instead of saying hello they'd greet me  by saying konichiwa  and they'd call me nicknames like jackie  chan  now in most cases these actions were  delivered as jokes rather than  ill-spirited racism  but regardless the message was clear  i didn't fit in  at first i was frustrated and confused  i collected pokemon cards just like  everyone else i watched spongebob just  like everyone else  i thought i was american just like  everyone else  eventually frustration and confusion  turned into darker feelings of  loneliness and self-doubt  i'm not good enough i told myself  i'm not white enough  i don't fit in  to combat these insecurities i began to  shun my asian-ness  i ignored my chinese teacher  i refused to eat sushi or used  chopsticks  i was even more excited to celebrate st  patrick's day than chinese new year  kinda crazy  but at the end of the day i still looked  asian  and so  the jokes still came  humans have two basic but powerful  psychological needs  the first is to fit in  the second is to stand out  we all want to feel a sense of belonging  and yet we all also want to feel special  a study conducted in 2019 found that  over 60 percent of americans report  feeling lonely  another survey found that 8 in 10  millennials believe  they aren't good enough  i felt disheartened hearing these  statistics  but they also reminded me that i am not  alone in feeling alone  when my grandfather who he called yaya  immigrated from china in 1951 he arrived  in the u.s with nothing and with no one  he told my dad that he often felt hungry  homesick  and alone  the language barrier made it nearly  impossible for him to connect  with other americans  but  while he was pursuing his masters at the  university of michigan he began to  develop an interest in american sports  he then moved to new york city where  that interest evolved into a lifelong  passion for several new york sports  teams  as a kid i remember him sporting a gray  new york giants crew neck sweatshirt  anytime he came to visit us  it took me several years to fully grasp  the importance of yaya's love for sports  but i now see how becoming a sports fan  helped him fit in  in 2004 my grandparents moved in with us  and for the next seven years  yaya didn't miss a single michigan  football game  he watched every single new york giants  yankees rangers and knicks game  why you might ask  because they reminded him of the  happiest moments of his life  the times when he felt like an insider  rather than an outsider  sports was a common bond that helped him  to transcend differences and connect  with his classmates colleagues and  grandchildren  today i want to propose a new solution  for conquering feelings of loneliness  and self-doubt  i want everyone here to consider sports  as a cure for the fear of disconnection  when we feel alone what we need most is  connection with others  in sports of a unique superpower that  brings people together regardless of  background  sports turn outsiders  into insiders  take for example how becoming a sports  fan instantly connects you with a  community  when we feel invested in a sport or team  or an athlete  we automatically feel a sense of  camaraderie with others with similar  affinities  sports psychologist dr daniel wan has  conducted multiple studies that have  found statistically significant  correlations between sports team  identification and several measures of  mental health  including self-esteem and life  satisfaction  and sports are accessible to anyone  there are no prerequisites no membership  fees and no application processes  sports also gave us a platform to build  deeper relationships  research conducted by professor brian  uzi at the kellogg school of management  found that the secret to building deep  relationships is shared experiences  and while there are many different types  of shared experiences sports are unique  in their inherent combination of  socialness  levity and authenticity  consider the fact that 95 of the top 100  most watched tv programs last year were  sports telecasts  or consider the fact that there are  thousands of sports bars around the  world  but have yet to come across any movie  bars or politics bars  has anyone here ever been to a weather  bar  yeah me neither  sports are built-in social events  that allow us to build and grow human  connection  in may 2012 i attended a college  lacrosse game that changed my life  forever  on that day i met an incredible girl  named amy  now on the surface amy and i had very  little in common  amy was a blond-haired blue-eyed  politics and philosophy major from rural  virginia  not how i'd describe myself  but on that day sports allowed us to  focus on the things we shared rather  than our differences  we shared our first meal together  a warm sugar-coated funnel cake from the  concession stand  and we cheered on our classmates from  the bleachers together  10 years later  and i can't wait to marry amy this fall  yet without sports we may have never  realized the potential for a fulfilling  lifelong relationship  and finally  sports enable us to embrace our  differences  consider serena williams who dove head  first into a sport lawn associated with  money and privilege  serena has gone on to win 23 grand slam  singles titles and is the only american  male or female to have won more than 20  majors  serena has become one of the greatest  athletes of all time  in a recent interview serena shared  i wouldn't be who i am  i wouldn't be as strong as i am  i wouldn't have been able to be as  amazing as i've been so far in my career  i wouldn't be me  if i wasn't black  being asian in america is hard  being a minority in america is hard  you can't help but think about the  things that make you different  every single  day  but as sports have helped me to feel  more connected to the world  i've been able to reframe those  differences  i wouldn't have had the same life  experiences if i wasn't asian  i wouldn't have the same view of the  world if i wasn't asian  i wouldn't be standing here today if i  wasn't asian  if i wasn't asian i wouldn't be me  when sports help me to fit in  being asian empowers me to stand out  yiya spent 60 years in america before  passing in 2011 after a 10-year battle  with parkinson's disease  but his love for sports lives deep in  the hearts of my dad  my three younger brothers and myself  he taught us a universal language the  language of sports  that has helped us to build bridges to  our american side of being asian  american  i'm 16 years old playing running back  for my high school's varsity football  team  as i jog out of the locker room and onto  the field i no longer see slanted eyes  no one says kunichiwa  no one calls me jackie chan  instead i'm greeted with high fives and  chest bumps  and as i join my teammates in the huddle  on the field the quarterback jimmy  simply turns to me  nods and says  let's go brother  embracing sports is the single most  important thing i've done for myself  it's been my cheat code for fostering  connection and combating loneliness  here at the gsb  i have built valuable friendships on the  soccer field at maples pavilion  and at old pro the sports bar downtown  by giving me a home where i fit in  sports have given me the courage  to embrace my differences  and appreciate how special it is to be  asian american  i'm asian and i'm american  i fit in  and i stand out  so the next time you feel alone or like  you're not good enough  find a sport or a team or an athlete  that interests you  it could be one of stanford's 36 varsity  sports teams  or could be your daughter's little  league team  and then go watch a game  you never know  who you might meet  [Applause]  [Music]

